✅ Theory for Email Spam Detection Using Binary Classification (KNN & SVM)
1. Introduction

Email spam detection is a binary classification problem where the goal is to automatically determine whether an email is:

Normal State → Not Spam (label = 0)

Abnormal State → Spam (label = 1)

Machine learning models can learn patterns from email text (words, frequency, structure) and classify future emails accordingly. In this practical, we use two supervised ML algorithms:

K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN)

Support Vector Machine (SVM)

✅ 2. Pre-processing the Dataset

The emails.csv dataset contains email content along with spam labels. Pre-processing steps ensure the text can be used by ML models.

Steps:
(a) Remove Missing or Duplicate Values

Cleaning ensures that only valid rows are used.

(b) Text Normalization

Convert raw email into structured numerical form:

Convert text to lowercase

Remove punctuation, numbers, and special characters

Remove stopwords (e.g., “the”, “and”, “is”)

Apply stemming or lemmatization

(c) Feature Extraction (Vectorization)

Since ML models require numerical data, text is converted using:

Bag-of-Words (BoW)

TF-IDF (Term Frequency – Inverse Document Frequency) ← most commonly used

TF-IDF gives weight to important words while reducing the influence of common ones.

(d) Train-Test Split

Usually:
70% training data + 30% testing data

✅ 3. Implementing Classification Models
✅ A. K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN)
Concept:

KNN classifies an email by looking at its ‘K’ closest neighbors in the vector space.
If most neighbors are spam → email is classified as spam.

Characteristics:

Simple and intuitive

Works well for smaller datasets

Computationally expensive for large text datasets

Sensitive to feature scaling

Key Parameter:

K (number of neighbors): usually odd numbers like 3, 5, 7.

✅ B. Support Vector Machine (SVM)
Concept:

SVM finds the best hyperplane that separates spam and non-spam emails with the maximum margin.

Characteristics:

Very effective in high-dimensional data like text

Handles large feature spaces well (due to TF-IDF vectors)

Robust to overfitting

Kernel trick improves accuracy

Common Kernel:

Linear Kernel (best for text classification)

RBF Kernel if non-linear separation is required

✅ 4. Model Evaluation
Metrics Used:

Accuracy
Portion of correctly predicted emails.

Precision
Out of the emails predicted as spam, how many were actually spam?

Recall
Out of all real spam emails, how many did the model detect?

F1-Score
Harmonic mean of precision and recall; best for imbalanced datasets.

Confusion Matrix
Summarizes TP, TN, FP, FN.

✅ 5. Performance Comparison
✅ Expected Observations
K-Nearest Neighbors

Moderate accuracy

Slow for large datasets

Performance depends greatly on value of K

Sensitive to scaling and high dimensionality

Support Vector Machine

Usually higher accuracy than KNN

Works extremely well with TF-IDF

Faster and more efficient with large text data

Often achieves highest precision in spam detection tasks

✅ Conclusion

SVM generally performs better than KNN for email spam classification because:

Text datasets have high dimensionality

SVM handles large feature spaces efficiently

SVM provides better generalization and boundary separation

Thus, SVM is preferred for real-world spam detection systems.

✅ Short Viva Questions (Useful in Practical Exam)
Q1. What is binary classification?

A task that classifies data into two categories (spam/not spam).

Q2. Why do we use TF-IDF?

To convert text into numerical features while giving more weight to important words.

Q3. Why does SVM perform better for text data?

Because text has high-dimensional sparse vectors and SVM handles them efficiently.

Q4. What is K in KNN?

K represents the number of nearest neighbors used for decision-making.

Q5. What is the confusion matrix?

A table showing TP, TN, FP, FN to evaluate model performance.

Q6. What is the main weakness of KNN?

It becomes slow and inaccurate with large, high-dimensional datasets like emails.
